      <floats-group id="article-142918.s9">
        <fig id="article-142918.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Types of Field Defects in Glaucoma. This image illustrates field defects indicative of glaucoma (early-onset glaucoma to advanced disease). Contributed by S Ruia, MS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="glaucomatous__field__defect300" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-142918.s10">
        <fig id="article-142918.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Types of Visual Field Defects in Other Diseases. The common field defect patterns documented in the optic neuritis treatment trial were diffuse field loss, altitudinal field defect, central scotoma or centrocecal scotoma, arcuate or double arcuate defects, and hemianopia defects. Contributed by S Ruia, MS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="visual__field__defects__in__other__diseases300" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-142918.s11">
        <fig id="article-142918.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Humphrey Visual Field Printout (24-2 Test) With Zones. The automated perimetry report for Humphrey can be divided into 8 zones for ease of understanding. Contributed by K Tripathy, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="HVF__printout__zones" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-142918.s12">
        <fig id="article-142918.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Humphrey Visual Field Printout (10-2 Test). Due to the 6&#x000b0; spacing between points, paracentral scotomas may be missed on 24-2 or 30-2 programs; any defect near fixation on these programs should be reevaluated using the 10-2 program. Contributed by K Tripathy, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="HVF__10-2__printout" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-142918.s13">
        <fig id="article-142918.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Fixation Targets in Humphrey Field Analyzer. The fixation target may be a central dot, the default target for the Humphrey visual field analyzer, or a large diamond below the central dot. Contributed by K Tripathy, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Fixation__patterns__in__Humphrey__field__analyzer" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
